// Salt dissolving in water, written as an item document (docs/item-document.md). The chemistry kit builds the rock-salt
// lattice from its lattice constant and ionic radii, and dissolves corner ions with water molecules turned the right way round.
Item(
    id: "salt-dissolving",
    title: "Salt dissolving in water",
    subject: Chemistry,
    idea: "Salt is a lattice of ions; water pulls the ions apart and surrounds each one.",
    reference: "To be confirmed by the author: textbook account of sodium chloride as an ionic lattice (each ion surrounded by six of the other kind) and of dissolving as water molecules surrounding and separating the ions (oxygen ends towards Na+, hydrogen ends towards Cl-). Lattice constant, ionic radii and hydration distances are standard published values.",
    objects: [
        Crystal(id: "salt", kind: NaCl, edge: 4),
    ],
    story: [
        Beat(id: Some("crystal"),
             say: "This is a tiny piece of a crystal of salt, sodium chloride (NaCl). It is made of ions packed tightly together: sodium ions, Na⁺, and chloride ions, Cl⁻. One grain of table salt holds about a billion billion of them.",
             label: ["salt"]),
        Beat(id: Some("grid"),
             say: "To see the pattern, the ions are now drawn smaller. They sit on a regular cubic grid, sodium and chloride taking turns in every direction. The lines only show the grid: they are not bonds.",
             unlabel: ["salt"],
             do: [OpenUp("salt")],
             label: ["salt.lines"]),
        Beat(id: Some("neighbours"),
             say: "Look at one sodium ion. Six chloride ions surround it: above, below, in front, behind, left and right. Each chloride ion is surrounded by six sodium ions in the same way. Opposite charges attract, and this pull in every direction holds the crystal together. This is ionic bonding.",
             point_at: ["salt.a-sodium-ion"]),
        Beat(id: Some("count"),
             say: "Count them: 32 sodium ions and 32 chloride ions. There is always one sodium ion for each chloride ion, so the formula is NaCl.",
             unlabel: ["salt.a-sodium-ion", "salt.lines"]),
        Beat(id: Some("sodium"),
             say: "Now the crystal is put into water. Water molecules have a slightly negative oxygen end and a slightly positive hydrogen end. They crowd round a corner ion, held by only three neighbours, and pull it off. Six water molecules surround the sodium ion, oxygen ends facing it.",
             do: [Dissolve(crystal: "salt", ions: 1)]),
        Beat(id: Some("chloride"),
             say: "A chloride ion is pulled away in the same way. Here the water molecules turn their hydrogen ends towards the ion.",
             do: [Dissolve(crystal: "salt", ions: 1)],
             label: ["salt.corner-1", "salt.corner-2"]),
        Beat(id: Some("more"),
             say: "Ion by ion, starting at the corners and edges, the crystal comes apart.",
             do: [Dissolve(crystal: "salt", ions: 2)]),
        Beat(id: Some("solution"),
             say: "Given enough water, the whole crystal comes apart like this: the salt dissolves. The ions are not destroyed. They spread through the water as separate Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions, each surrounded by water molecules. This is a salt solution."),
    ],
)
